  "final_message": "I haven't changed anything, because `channel` is still read in a few places and removing it would break other code.\n\n- **`analytics-ingest` needs it from the event.** `analytics-ingest/src/core/events.ts:34` reads `channel` as a required field on `OrderPlaced`. If it's missing, ingestion rejects the whole event with a `ContractViolationError` (see `analytics-ingest/test/ingest.test.ts:88` and `:158`). It also writes `channel` into the `orders` table (`analytics-ingest/src/ingest.ts:17`). If we drop it from the event, every `OrderPlaced` would be rejected by that service.\n- **`orders-service` reads it from the order model.** `src/api/legacyExport.ts:6` and `:18` put `order.channel` in the legacy export, which has a `channel` column in its header. A test asserts that header (`test/adapters.test.ts:137`).\n- **Input validation also uses it.** `createOrder` validates `channel` (`src/core/order.ts:124`), and `POST /orders` takes it as a request field. That field appears in `CLAUDE.md` and the README example.\n- **`billing-service` doesn't need it.** Its tests only set `channel` in a sample event, so it looks safe.\n\n`analytics-ingest` belongs to another team and is read-only here. Dropping the field from the event would need that team to stop requiring it first.\n\nHow do you want to proceed? These are the options I see:\n1. Remove `channel` from the order model only. That means updating the legacy export (dropping its column), validation, request parsing and tests. The event would keep `channel`, which would have to be derived from somewhere else, so this probably doesn't work on its own.\n2. Remove it everywhere in `orders-service`, on the understanding that `analytics-ingest` will be changed first or at the same time.\n3. Leave it as is.",
